How many times do we have to say that, before you understand it? We
aren't blindly running into combat. Projectiles are important resources
that should be used on threatening monsters. Throwing projectiles at
trash mobs, when you aren't sure if you need projectiles in the near
future, is poor resource management.

For example, if I'm in the Gnomish Mines, and I can't tell what
monsters are outside of my light source, then I'm assuming there might
be a mumak on the level. Therefore, I won't throw projectiles at the
trash mobs, because I really want to have projectiles in case a mumak
does show up.

It is acceptable to throw projectiles at monsters when you are
completely sure that you won't need them in the near future, but I'd
still argue that it's generally uneccessary unless that character can
also build up skill in that projectile. For example, it might be good
for rogues to throw daggers at trash mobs, but it's generally
uneccessary for Monks to throw projectiles at trash mobs, because they
have more than enough HP to easily soak up the weak damage of the trash
mobs.

A notable exception is the Wizard, because both of their main attacks
(projectiles and magic) are ranged.  This means that even when it's
very early in the game, you don't have to worry about projectile
management as much as the other characters, because they can always
switch to an attack spell (i.e. force bolt) if something threatening
appears.



